 "Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear. Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear. Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear. Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "naive", "type": "name"}, {"content": "unaffected; innocent ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Of course I was a little naive.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le16ob11aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are words to review.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "novel", "type": "name"}, {"content": "new; unusual ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: They would have a hard time adjusting to the novel face-to-face concept.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le16ob11aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "payphone", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a \u200bpublic \u200btelephone operated by putting \u200bcoins into it or by using a \u200bspecial \u200bcard", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Payphones are available on the street if you need to contact someone while you are out. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le16ob11aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "answering machine", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a \u200bdevice \u200bconnected to a \u200btelephone that \u200banswers \u200bautomatically and \u200brecords \u200bmessages", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Answering machines are rare. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le16ob11aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "World Wide Web", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a computer network used for storing and exchanging information; the Internet", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: If you consider that his work was published long before the World Wide Web, it is quite astonishing.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le16ob11aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Real and Unreal Conditionals", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the Real Conditional to express events or situations that are likely to happen.<br/>You use the Unreal Conditional to talk about hypothetical situations and events, or outcomes that are unlikely to be true.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">Unless</span> is used in conditional sentences as a necessary condition without which the outcome of the sentence can't happen.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">If ... not</span> is used when the outcome in the <span class=\"italic\">if clause</span> is unexpected or in questions.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use <span class=\"italic\">if ... happen to</span>, or <span class=\"italic\">if ... should happen to</span> to express an outcome that may be possible, but is not very likely.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use the Unreal Conditional to talk about a hypothetical or unlikely situation. When you want to be more formal you can use <span class=\"italic\">were</span> + subject + <span class=\"italic\">to</span> infinitive.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use <span class=\"italic\">if</span> or <span class=\"italic\">whether</span> with the Unreal Conditional to say that the possible outcomes of a hypothetical condition have been thought about, or to suggest that you are unsure about something. They are usually interchangeable.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">Whether</span> can be followed immediately by <span class=\"italic\">or not</span>. <br/> <span class=\"italic\">I'm not sure whether or not he'll come.</span></br>When you use <span class=\"italic\">if</span>, <span class=\"italic\">... or not</span> is at the end of the sentence.</br><span class=\"italic\">I'm not sure if he'll come or not.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "If you remember the time before mobile phones were invented, you know that people had no choice but to interact. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If you should happen to be with a group of people, look around.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If you were to try to converse, what would happen?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You'll be naive if you don't agree. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": " If you were to take their phones away, they would have a hard time adjusting to the novel face-to-face concept.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I don't know whether I will live long enough to see this.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Future Perfect: Simple and Continuous ", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the Future Perfect tense to talk about an action in the future before another action in the future. This is the past in the future.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You can think of the Future Perfect tense as being similar to the Present Perfect tense, but instead of your viewpoint being in the present, it is in the future.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Structure: <br/><span class=\"italic\">will have</span> + past participle", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example: <br/><span class=\"italic\">By the time they are adults most children will have carried phones with them for years.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When you use the <span class=\"strong\">Future Perfect Continuous</span>, you can sometimes think of it as the Past Continuous, but instead of your viewpoint being in the past, it is in the future. <br/>With the Future Perfect Continuous, an action in the future is unfinished by the time another action in the future starts. It is most often used with an expression of time.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Structure: <br/>subject + <span class=\"italic\">will have been</span> + present participle", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example:  <br/><span class=\"italic\">They will have been living in their heads focused on their screens for a long time. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "In the future we will have moved into even more connectivity.   ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We will have become part of the machine, like it or not.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Another will be having their attention drawn to a news item or Twitter feed. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "No one will have been talking to each other.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "In a group, talk about how interfacing with technology will change in the next 20 years if things keep progressing as they have been.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Talk about what people will be doing, will have done and will have been doing by that time with their technology.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write down predictions about how society will have changed because of cell phones and other technology in 20 years' time.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Read your prediction to a partner.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Discuss your ideas.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I talk about how interfacing with technology will change in the next 20 years.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I use the Future Perfect, Future Continuous and the Future Perfect Continuous.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I use the Real Conditional.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share and discuss my ideas.", "type": "text"}]}, {"content": "engenn10le16ob11re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}], "type": "sena"}